          "target": "XnaEffectProcessor.TheRealCompilerAnswersWhatXnaAnswered"
        },
        "differentialEvidence": "tests/reference/xna40/graphics/graphics-content-oracle.json effectprocessor/*, tests/reference/xna40/graphics/effect-compile-simple.bin",
        "windows": "SUPPORTED",
        "windowsPhone": "UNVERIFIED",
        "xbox360": "UNVERIFIED",
        "status": "IMPLEMENTED+TESTED",
        "note": "The route is measured against the genuine EffectProcessor with the compiler XNA itself used, Microsoft's legacy fxc at fx_2_0 from the June 2010 DirectX SDK, run through Wine. Two things came out of it. First, a real defect: a path handed to a compiler through a launcher was spelled the host's way, so fxc read '/tmp/.../effect.fxb' as an option and the whole .fx route was broken on every non-Windows machine; paths are now spelled the way the launcher's program reads them. Second, a measurement nothing in this repository had: XNA's compiled effect is a 0xBCF00BCF header in front of the Direct3D 9 Effect Framework binary, and the header is not fixed -- it is one pair of dwords per pass, so 8 + 8 x passCount bytes. The first of each pair is a bitmask of the device state the pass assigns (1 blend, 2 depth-stencil, 4 rasterizer, with the second copy shifted up three places) and the second a bitmask of the sampler registers the pass binds. Measuring it from the processor called directly gave the sixteen bytes this row used to claim, which is what a single-pass effect happens to produce; a real build says otherwise, and CNA emits the per-pass form now (XNAPP-191). What still differs is the blob behind that header: XNA compiles from a memory buffer through d3dx9 and CNA runs the fxc command line. Over seventeen effects every differing byte is accounted for -- 195 inside the compiler's own version string and 54 of padding d3dx9 leaves as heap residue -- and neither is reproducible without running Microsoft's compiler in-process. A relative source name and a working directory do not close it; only calling D3DXCreateEffectCompiler on a buffer would, which needs a native helper (XNAPP-191/192). sourceToCnb is a documented refusal rather than a gap: a .cnb carrying Direct3D 9 bytecode would be unloadable on every CNA renderer that is not Direct3D 9. The differential skips on a machine with no fxc -- set CNA_FXC to one -- so this row's coverage is real here and conditional elsewhere, which no other row is."
      }
    },
